The key product decision changed after the deeper research: DomainArena should not primarily answer “which domain do humans like?” Arena.ai itself has moved agent evaluation beyond preference votes toward confirmed task success, tool behavior, cost, recovery and causal real-world outcomes.

DomainArena should make the same transition for domain names.

For a machine-facing API or MCP tool, the hierarchy can genuinely become roughly:

semantic understanding → agent selection → successful invocation → task success → stability across model families → reuse → economics

Human aesthetics may be a tiny component. For a consumer brand, that changes: human recall, pronunciation, spelling, trust and brand elasticity become major objectives again. So the system in the pack has audience-conditioned policies, rather than one universal “domain quality score.”

That also makes the name.com story considerably stronger. name.com already has the rails for agents to search, register, manage domains and DNS, and their current developer strategy explicitly focuses on domain experiences that convert. Railway is already doing 1,700+ registrations/month through embedded name.com purchasing, so another autonomous-domain buyer isn't novel.

The missing piece is:

Given this product, this audience and this budget, which domain from name.com's actual inventory should the agent buy?

And name.com's API provides exactly the variables needed to make that optimization real: purchasability, premium status, purchase price, renewal price and purchase type. Search results are explicitly unordered, which is another reason there's room for an intelligent ranking layer on top.

